// If you recall from '09_modules/', a module is a directory which contains '*.glos' files.
// All those files in that directory are parsed and combined to produce a singular module
// associated with that directory.
//
// The sole exception to this rule is 'first.glos'. Instead of being bundled into the module,
// it gets immediately executed as a subprocess.
//
// In simple words, imagine that the compiler does
//
//     glos -R foo/first.glos
//
// right before parsing the module 'foo'.

// I think it is pretty self explanatory what this function does.
main :: () {
    println("Creating foo/constant.glos")

    f := os.create_file("constant.glos")!
    defer f.close()

    wprintln(f, "FOO :: 69")
}

// It should make sense now, where the 'foo/constant.glos' file is coming from, and why no
// "Undefined identifier" error is thrown for 'FOO'.

// Now head over to "bar/first.glos". There is one more concept left to be covered.

os :: #import "os"
